  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe flow pattern over the northern CONUS is still expected to\namplify over the next 48 hours. The strong ridge centered over\nthe southern Mississippi Valley should shift west to the Plains\nthrough the weekend, in response to an upper low dropping into\nthe Canadian Maritimes, dropping a trough down the Eastern\nSeaboard. Despite this, upper-level northwest flow continues\ninto Monday across our region.\n\nClouds and showers have helped to suppress destabilization so \nfar near and north of I-70. Current mesoanalysis supports this \nwith 1000+ J/kg of MLCAPE confined to areas south of Pittsburgh.\nThink that this is where the better severe threat lies today, \nwhere more significant and longer-lasting cloud breaks are \nlikely. A crossing subtle mid-level wave will continue to \nprovide the lift to support convection. Although deep shear is \nrelatively modest, short line segments/bows are possible in this\nregion, with damaging wind gusts remaining the primary severe \nthreat. Storms that can punch strong cores aloft will have 900+ \nJ/kg of DCAPE to work with as well, so a downburst wind threat \nexists. Hail is a secondary threat - 50 dBZ cores will likely \nhave to exceed 35kft for quarter-sized.\n\nThe flooding threat is also a concern through tonight. Have\nalready issued a Flood Watch near and south of the Pittsburgh\nmetro area. The corridor from Columbiana County southeast to\nWestmoreland County remains particularly wet, with 2 to 4 inch\nrainfall totals common from yesterday. Although instability in\nthis area should remain weaker than areas to the south,\nefficient rainfall is still expected from any storms, with\nprecipitable water of 1.7 to 1.8 inches progged. With 1-hour\nflash flood guidance values still below an inch, it will not\ntake much rain for renewed flooding issues. \n\nAfter this afternoon's storms pull east of the region, there is\nthe potential for another round of storms to fire to our \nnorthwest and ride into our region overnight. CAMs still largely\nadvertise this activity to some degree but remain inconsistent\non timing and coverage, so uncertainty does remain. The deeper \nmoisture/higher PWAT air starts to get suppressed to the south \nand west with time tonight as the east coast trough starts to \ndig and upper low slowly gains a more northerly component, but \nvalues remain elevated in eastern Ohio/northern West Virginia \nthrough the night. Thus, even though FFG is higher in these \nareas, wanted to cover these areas with the Flood Watch given \nthe multiple rounds of storms. This is supported by the 12Z \nHREF, which shows several potential maximum 24-hour \nprecipitation bands of 2 to 3 inches over the Flood Watch area \nthrough 8 AM Saturday. A severe threat may continue overnight as\nwell, especially if storms can combine cold pools and present a\ncontinued wind threat.\n\nBy the time storms depart to our south Saturday morning, a cold\nfront should begin making its way into the area from the north.\nSome additional showers and storms could fire along the front\nduring the afternoon, mainly along/south of I-70. instability\nprogs and machine-learning guidance suggest that any severe\nthreat would be largely confined to areas south of the Mason-\nDixon Line. Behind the front, drier air and more seasonable \ntemperatures return through the rest of the weekend and into \nearly next week.",
